  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q19-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "19",
    "prompt": "In a school, there are $n$ students. In a social club, there are to be $m$ officers and the other members of the social club are regular members.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q19-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If the social club has $k$ members, find the number of ways for $m$ officers to be appointed within the school.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{n}{k}\\binom{k}{m}$.",
          "worked": "Choose the $k$ club members from the $n$ students in $\\binom{n}{k}$ ways. Choose the $m$ officers from these members in $\\binom{k}{m}$ ways.\n\nThe number of outcomes is\n$$\\binom{n}{k}\\binom{k}{m}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q19-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "By appointing $m$ officers from the school first, and then filling the social club with more regular members, show that\n$$\\binom{n}{m}\\binom{m}{m}+\\binom{n}{m+1}\\binom{m+1}{m}+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}\\binom{n}{m}$$\nis equal to $2^{n-m}\\binom{n}{m}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\binom{n}{m}\\binom{m}{m}+\\binom{n}{m+1}\\binom{m+1}{m}+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}\\binom{n}{m}=2^{n-m}\\binom{n}{m}$.\n\nChoose the officers first, then let each remaining student join or stay out; compare with the club-size counts in part (a).",
          "worked": "Choose the $m$ officers from the $n$ students in $\\binom{n}{m}$ ways. Each remaining student independently joins as a regular member or stays out. The $n-m$ students give $2^{n-m}$ membership choices, so there are $2^{n-m}\\binom{n}{m}$ outcomes.\n\nAlternatively, for each club size $k=m,m+1,\\ldots,n$, part (a) gives $\\binom{n}{k}\\binom{k}{m}$ outcomes. These cases cover every possible club exactly once.\n\nBoth methods count the same clubs with appointed officers, hence\n$$\\binom{n}{m}\\binom{m}{m}+\\binom{n}{m+1}\\binom{m+1}{m}+\\cdots+\\binom{n}{n}\\binom{n}{m}=2^{n-m}\\binom{n}{m}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 60
      }
    ]
  },
